import{_ as s,o as a,c as r,ao as t}from"./chunks/framework.DUdA5YZf.js";const o="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l1_gate.Cu-HS9Rt.svg",l="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l2_gate.BevPKBqV.svg",n="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l3_gate.CCTilWr3.svg",p="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_lapack_gate.9JV0TUt7.svg",c="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl1_gate.CdOFsV7J.svg",i="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl2_gate.ivl09eyW.svg",d="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl3_gate.DBjeIPLm.svg",_="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_clapack_gate.B7_m4lFv.svg",m="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l1.DYvSGurx.svg",h="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l2.Bo_KU56A.svg",g="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l3.DWSGaqG9.svg",v="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_lapack.BI0UxJrI.svg",u="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl1.B3O_tnLE.svg",f="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl2.biLeXDGj.svg",A="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl3.CdCMfJQS.svg",L="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_clapack.CV_8oT8r.svg",b="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l1_aocl.uhPwdcth.svg",B="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l2_aocl.Dl-Fi7IT.svg",S="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l3_aocl.DAlkfjPI.svg",P="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_lapack_aocl.Dln1RlAM.svg",j="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl1_aocl.C0Yiar6U.svg",O="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl2_aocl.phEP2khp.svg",w="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl3_aocl.CM_nG5Mh.svg",C="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_clapack_aocl.DufdLG9S.svg",k="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_dl1.CWdSfiFa.svg",T="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_dl2.BbdeOxhO.svg",y="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_dl3._O1K3Eza.svg",x="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_dlp.B7bE39vo.svg",M=JSON.parse('{"title":"Performance","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"performance.md","filePath":"performance.md","lastUpdated":null}'),q={name:"performance.md"};function D(F,e,V,E,I,G){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t('<h1 id="Performance" tabindex="-1">Performance <a class="header-anchor" href="#Performance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Performance {#Performance}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Each plot shows how fast PureBLAS is relative to a reference, against problem size. Higher is better and the dashed line is parity, so anything above it is a win. There is one curve per microarchitecture (Zen3 · AVX2, Zen4 · AVX-512, Zen5 · AVX-512) and one panel per operation, and the band around a curve is the q10–q90 spread of the pooled per-round ratios. Everything is single-threaded, Float64 and ComplexF64.</p><p>Roughly 87% of the measured cells are at or above <code>max(OpenBLAS, AOCL)</code>. The ones that are not are listed in <a href="./notes">Notes</a>, and most of them sit within a few percent.</p><p>For the numbers behind the plots, see <a href="https://github.com/el-oso/PureBLAS.jl/blob/master/bench/gen_table.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>bench/gen_table.md</code></a> against OpenBLAS and <a href="https://github.com/el-oso/PureBLAS.jl/blob/master/bench/gen_table_aocl.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>bench/gen_table_aocl.md</code></a> against AOCL. <a href="./methodology">Methodology</a> covers how the measurements are taken and how to read a cell; <a href="./notes">Notes</a> has the per-routine analysis and history.</p><h2 id="vs-the-gate" tabindex="-1">vs the gate <a class="header-anchor" href="#vs-the-gate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;vs the gate {#vs-the-gate}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These plots divide by whichever reference is faster at each point — <code>max(OpenBLAS, AOCL)</code>, the same number the coverage tables report. A point below the dashed line here is a real miss. The two sections after it show each library on its own, so a miss against AOCL can look like a comfortable win on the OpenBLAS plot; start here when you want to know where PureBLAS falls short.</p><p><img src="'+o+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+l+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+n+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+p+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+c+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+i+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+d+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+_+'" alt=""></p><h2 id="vs-OpenBLAS" tabindex="-1">vs OpenBLAS <a class="header-anchor" href="#vs-OpenBLAS" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;vs OpenBLAS {#vs-OpenBLAS}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+m+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+h+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+g+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+v+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+u+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+f+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+A+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+L+'" alt=""></p><h2 id="vs-AOCL" tabindex="-1">vs AOCL <a class="header-anchor" href="#vs-AOCL" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;vs AOCL {#vs-AOCL}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+b+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+B+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+S+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+P+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+j+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+O+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+w+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+C+'" alt=""></p><h2 id="vs-LinearAlgebra-generic-dual-numbers" tabindex="-1">vs LinearAlgebra generic (dual numbers) <a class="header-anchor" href="#vs-LinearAlgebra-generic-dual-numbers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;vs LinearAlgebra generic (dual numbers) {#vs-LinearAlgebra-generic-dual-numbers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>One section, not two, because there is no second reference to compare against. <code>ForwardDiff.Dual{Tag,Float64,1}</code> is not a <code>BlasFloat</code>, so LinearAlgebra dispatches to its own generic fallback and no vendor BLAS is ever reached — OpenBLAS and AOCL have no dual-number path at all. The denominator here is therefore what a forward-mode AD user gets today <em>without</em> PureBLAS, and these ratios are <strong>not</strong> gate verdicts: the gate is <code>PB ≥ max(OpenBLAS, AOCL)</code>, which is undefined for this element type.</p><p><img src="'+k+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+T+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+y+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+x+'" alt=""></p><p>These numbers apply however you call PureBLAS. The native API (<code>PureBLAS.gemm!(…)</code>), the in-process reroute through <code>activate()</code>, and the <code>libpureblas.so</code> built for C hosts all run the same kernels.</p>',39)])])}const N=s(q,[["render",D]]);export{M as __pageData,N as default};
